5️⃣ Slowly Changing Dimensions (SCD)

Tracks changes in dimension attributes over time.

SCD Type 0 – Retain Original

What:
No changes allowed.

Healthcare Example:
Patient Date of Birth

SCD Type 1 – Overwrite

What:
Old value is replaced; no history.

Healthcare Example:
Fixing spelling errors in patient name

SCD Type 2 – Full History

What:
New row created for every change.

Columns:
Effective_Date, Expiry_Date, Current_Flag

Healthcare Example:
Patient address, insurance plan

SCD Type 3 – Limited History

What:
Stores current and previous value.

Healthcare Example:
Previous and current provider department

SCD Type 4 – History Table

What:
Current data in main table, history in separate table.

Healthcare Example:
A provider can change specialization over time (e.g., General Medicine → Cardiology).

SCD Type 6 – Hybrid (1 + 2 + 3)

What:
Combination of overwrite + history + previous value.

Healthcare Example:
Patient address tracked historically with current and previous values available
---------------------------------------
✅ Slowly Changing Dimension (SCD) Type 3 — Explained Simply (with Example)

SCD Type 3 is used when you want to keep limited history, usually only the previous value, not full historical tracking.

📌 What does SCD Type 3 store?

Current value

Previous value

No unlimited history (only one level of history)

🏢 Business Example (Very Common)
Scenario: Employee Department Change

HR wants to know:

Current department of an employee

Previous department (only the last one)

They do NOT want full department change history.

🧱 Dimension Table Structure (SCD Type 3)
dim_employee
------------
emp_id
emp_name
current_dept
previous_dept

📊 Example Data
Before Change
emp_id	emp_name	current_dept	previous_dept
101	John	IT	NULL
Employee Moves from IT → HR
After Update (Type 3)
emp_id	emp_name	current_dept	previous_dept
101	John	HR	IT

👉 Only one previous value is stored.
