Advanced Facts

1️⃣ Additive Facts
What

Additive facts are measures that can be summed across all dimensions (time, location, product, patient, etc.) without losing meaning.

Why
Simplest and most common type of facts
Easy aggregation and reporting

Where Used
Transaction fact tables
Snapshot fact tables

Examples
Number of Orders
Website Traffic (Visits)
Employee Hours Worked

Healthcare Example
Number of patient visits
Total claim amount
Total hours worked by nurses

Scenario

In our healthcare DW, we summed total patient visits and billing amounts across date, facility, and department to generate monthly operational and financial reports.

Memory Tip

If you can SUM it across everything, it’s additive.

2️⃣ Semi-Additive Facts
What

Semi-additive facts can be summed across some dimensions, but not across time (usually).

Why

Represents point-in-time values
Time aggregation can distort meaning

Where Used
Periodic snapshot fact tables

Examples
Inventory Levels
Patient Bed Occupancy
Number of employees on a specific day

Healthcare Example
Beds occupied per day
Patients in ICU at midnight

Scenario

We used patient bed occupancy as a semi-additive fact—summed across departments and facilities, but not across dates, to avoid overstating hospital capacity.

Memory Tip

Add it across space, not across time.

3️⃣ Non-Additive Facts
What

Non-additive facts cannot be summed across any dimension because doing so produces incorrect results.

Why
Represents ratios, percentages, or calculated values
Requires recalculation, not summation

Where Used
Fact tables with derived metrics
BI layer calculations

Examples
Average values
Percentages
Geographic coordinates

Healthcare Example
Average length of stay
Readmission rate (%)
Patient satisfaction score (%)

Scenario

In healthcare quality dashboards, metrics like readmission rate and average length of stay were calculated dynamically instead of being summed, ensuring accurate reporting at different aggregation levels.
